<%- include('partials/generator') %>

<div class="wrap prose">
  <h2 id="name">Is it still called a tax invoice?</h2>
  <p>Not in the law any more. From 1 April 2023 the requirement to use tax invoices was replaced by a requirement to provide and keep <strong>taxable supply information</strong>. A debit or credit note became <strong>supply correction information</strong>. The rules for what GST you charge did not change.</p>
  <p>IRD says you do not need to change the wording of the GST documents you use, and you may keep giving taxable supply information in one document marked "tax invoice". This template keeps the heading (number 1 in the example). Other countries, such as the UK, still ask for a VAT invoice with its own list of details: see <a href="/blog/what-is-vat-invoice">what is a VAT invoice</a>. In New Zealand the information can also sit in several records, such as a contract and a bank statement, and sellers can send it by automated exchange between software, for example PEPPOL eInvoicing.</p>
  <p class="source">Sources: <a href="<%= IRD.tsi %>" rel="noopener">IRD, Taxable supply information for GST</a> (updated 27 November 2024) and <a href="<%= IRD.works %>" rel="noopener">How taxable supply information for GST works</a> (updated 31 March 2026). Checked <%= checked %>.</p>

  <h2 id="requirements">What a New Zealand tax invoice must show</h2>
  <p>What you must show depends on the amount of the supply. The numbers in brackets refer to the example above. Number 1 is the heading, which is optional.</p>
  <table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>Detail</th><th>$200 or less</th><th>Over $200, up to $1,000</th><th>Over $1,000</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="strong">Seller (2, 3)</td><td>Name or trade name</td><td>Name or trade name, and GST number</td><td>Name or trade name, and GST number</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="strong">Buyer (4)</td><td>Not required</td><td>Not required</td><td>If the buyer is GST registered: its name, and one or more of address, phone number, email address, trading name if different, NZBN or website</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="strong">Date (5)</td><td>Date of invoice, or the time of supply if there is no invoice</td><td>The same</td><td>The same</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="strong">What you supplied (6)</td><td>Description of the goods or services</td><td>The same</td><td>The same</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="strong">Amounts (7, 8, 9)</td><td>The price</td><td>Either the GST-exclusive amount, the GST amount and the GST-inclusive amount, or the GST-inclusive amount with a statement that GST is included, charged at the standard rate on all items</td><td>The same as the middle column</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="source">Sources: <a href="<%= IRD.works %>" rel="noopener">IRD, How taxable supply information for GST works</a> and <a href="<%= IRD.guide %>" rel="noopener">IRD GST guide IR375</a> (March 2026). Checked <%= checked %>. Imported goods and services and secondhand goods have their own lists on those pages.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>$200 or less, including GST:</strong> you do not have to give the buyer anything. You and a registered buyer each keep your own record of the sale.</li>
    <li><strong>Over $200:</strong> give it to a GST-registered buyer within 28 days of a request, or by a date you agree. Give it once. If the buyer loses it, send a copy, and you do not need to mark it as a copy.</li>
    <li><strong>Amounts in NZD.</strong> IRD's guide says amounts of money must be expressed in New Zealand currency.</li>
    <li><strong>Your GST number.</strong> The guide tells you to give it to customers as part of the information.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="gst">GST at 15%: adding it and taking it out</h2>
  <p>The rate of GST is 15%. To add it, multiply the price by 0.15. To take it out of a price that includes GST, multiply the total by 3, divide by 23, and subtract the result. IRD calls this the fraction method and says it always gives an accurate answer.</p>
  <div class="formula">Adding GST: $4,300.00 x 0.15 = $645.00 GST<br><b>$4,300.00 + $645.00 = $4,945.00 including GST</b><br>Taking GST out: $4,945.00 x 3 / 23 = $645.00 GST, so $4,300.00 before GST<br>IRD's own example: $115 including GST holds $15 of GST. $100 including GST holds $13.04.</div>
  <p>For the same sums on a UK, Dutch or other VAT invoice, see <a href="/how-to-calculate-vat-on-invoice">how to calculate VAT on an invoice</a>.</p>
  <p class="source">Sources: <a href="<%= IRD.calc %>" rel="noopener">IRD, Calculating your GST</a> (updated 14 April 2025), <a href="<%= IRD.charging %>" rel="noopener">Charging GST</a> (updated 1 April 2026) and the <a href="<%= IRD.guide %>" rel="noopener">GST guide IR375</a>. Checked <%= checked %>.</p>

  <h2 id="register">Do you need a GST number?</h2>
  <p>You must register for GST when you carry out a taxable activity and your turnover was at least $60,000 in the last 12 months, or you expect it to be in the next 12 months. You must also register if you add GST to your prices, whatever your turnover. Below $60,000 you can register voluntarily.</p>
  <p>IRD words the limit two ways: its registering page says "at least $60,000" and its March 2026 GST guide says "over $60,000". If your turnover is close to the line, ask IRD before you decide. The registration confirmation gives your GST number, which may be the same as your IRD number.</p>
  <p>Only a registered business can charge GST. If you are not registered, send an ordinary invoice with no GST line and no GST number.</p>
  <p class="source">Sources: <a href="<%= IRD.register %>" rel="noopener">IRD, Registering for GST</a> (updated 13 February 2025) and the <a href="<%= IRD.guide %>" rel="noopener">GST guide IR375</a>. Checked <%= checked %>.</p>

  <h2 id="export">Clients overseas: zero-rated supplies</h2>
  <p>Some supplies are charged at 0% GST. They still go on the invoice, shown at 0% GST, and they go in a separate box on your GST return. IRD lists these among the exported services and goods:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Services supplied to non-residents outside New Zealand when you perform them.</li>
    <li>Remote services to non-residents. You must show the customer is not a New Zealand resident, using your usual checks or any 2 of: billing address, IP address or other geolocation, bank details, mobile country code of the SIM, or fixed landline location.</li>
    <li>Services performed outside New Zealand.</li>
    <li>Goods you export. Goods entered for export must leave within 28 days of the time of supply, unless IRD agrees to more time.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The list has many more cases and conditions, so check the page for yours. Wording for other countries is in <a href="/blog/how-to-invoice-international-clients">how to invoice international clients</a>. If you also invoice in Australia, see the <a href="/free-invoice-generator-australia">Australian GST invoice guide</a>.</p>
  <p class="source">Source: <a href="<%= IRD.zero %>" rel="noopener">IRD, Zero-rated supplies</a> (updated 22 June 2026). Checked <%= checked %>.</p>

  <h2 id="fix">Correcting an invoice: supply correction information</h2>
  <p>Give supply correction information when the first document had the wrong GST amount, or the sale changed after you sent it. It does not matter whether the buyer has paid. Cases include a cancelled sale, goods sent back, a wrong description, wrong buyer details, a wrong date, GST at the wrong rate or a wrong GST amount. It must show:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>your name or trade name and your GST number,</li>
    <li>the date you gave the correction,</li>
    <li>details that identify the original, such as the invoice number,</li>
    <li>the correction, including any change to the GST charged.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You and the buyer both keep it. You can put a correction for an earlier sale in the same document as a new invoice. For how to number invoices and their corrections, see <a href="/blog/invoice-number-format">invoice number format</a>.</p>
  <p class="source">Sources: <a href="<%= IRD.correction %>" rel="noopener">IRD, Supply correction information</a> (updated 1 April 2023) and the <a href="<%= IRD.guide %>" rel="noopener">GST guide IR375</a> (page 18). Checked <%= checked %>.</p>

  <h2 id="records">Records</h2>
  <p>Keep all your records, including electronic ones, for at least 7 tax years. Records must be in English or Māori unless IRD approves another language. If you keep records overseas, including in the cloud, you or your provider needs IRD's approval. Source: <a href="<%= IRD.records %>" rel="noopener">IRD, Record keeping</a> (updated 14 May 2026). Checked <%= checked %>.</p>

  <h2 id="wording">Wording to copy</h2>
  <div class="copy"><div><small>Heading, as IRD allows</small><span class="txt">TAX INVOICE</span></div><button type="button" aria-label="Copy the heading">Copy</button></div>
  <div class="copy"><div><small>For a total over $200 that shows only the GST-inclusive amount</small><span class="txt">All prices include GST at 15%, the standard rate, on every item listed.</span></div><button type="button" aria-label="Copy the GST-inclusive statement">Copy</button></div>
  <div class="copy"><div><small>A zero-rated export of services</small><span class="txt">GST at 0%: zero-rated services supplied to a non-resident outside New Zealand.</span></div><button type="button" aria-label="Copy the zero-rated wording">Copy</button></div>
  <div class="copy"><div><small>A supply correction (example figures)</small><span class="txt">Supply correction for invoice INV-0042 dated 3 November 2026: hours reduced from 20 to 18. Amount reduced by $190.00 plus GST of $28.50, a total of $218.50.</span></div><button type="button" aria-label="Copy the supply correction wording">Copy</button></div>
  <div class="copy"><div><small>Payment line</small><span class="txt">Payment within 14 days by bank transfer to 00-0000-0000000-00, quoting the invoice number. All amounts are in NZD.</span></div><button type="button" aria-label="Copy the payment line">Copy</button></div>

